Hmm, both a and b printed at ~276.609 and .611 — again nearly the same. So the reads did not block at all, meaning all of stdin was slurped upfront (at t≈0.609). Wait, but the producer sleeps 0.5s before writing 'a'. So the slurp read completed at ~1.0s? Then a printed at 1.0... but the timestamp is 276.609 — what was the start time? I don't know. The two timestamps 2ms apart means both were processed back-to-back after blocking read finished.

Hmm, but if it slurped all of stdin, then the first output would come after 1.0s of blocking, and both outputs 2ms apart. That's consistent!

If it read incrementally, first output at 0.5s, second at 1.0s — timestamps 500ms apart. Not observed.

So: slurp all of stdin upfront (blocking until EOF), then iterate.

But then why does empty input loop forever? Let me test the case of `printf 'a\nb\n'` again vs `printf ''`... 

OH WAIT. Maybe I have it backwards. Maybe it reads lines incrementally with a BufReader, and the loop with 1us delay runs so fast... no, blocking reads would still block.

Hmm, but wait: if it read incrementally and blocked on read, then output would be: a printed at ~0.5s... unless the loop reads the NEXT line BEFORE printing the current item. Let's model: iteration 1: read line (blocks until 0.5s) → item=a → run cmd. Iteration 2: read line (blocks until 1.0s) → item=b → run cmd. Iteration 3: read → EOF → ??? So the item would be printed AFTER the read. Then a would print at 0.5s and b at 1.0s → 500ms apart. Not observed.

So slurp-upfront is right. Then, the empty case loops forever...

Unless it's not a slurp but rather: it reads a chunk... hmm.

Let me think about a different model: maybe the loop reads stdin lazily but the "every" timer drives the loop, and it spawns the command asynchronously? No.

New idea: maybe the implementation reads stdin into a Vec, and iterates; when the Vec is exhausted, it just continues looping (with the last item) — but that contradicts stopping after 2.

